WALK = [(0, None, [('barinov', 'Не'), ('barinov', 'надо'), ' \'Do not look at me like that.\' This phrase, "not needed", works here as "don\'t", followed by the dictionary form, "to look". This phrase is "at me". He says it to someone in the room.']), (1, None, [('barinov', 'любых'), ' \'Now money is more important than any silly principles.\' This word is "any at all". This phrase has no "than" word: this word becomes this word, and that ending does the work.']), (2, None, [('barinov', 'выигрыш'), ' \'With these winnings I will buy food.\' This word, "winnings", is this word, "play", with this starting bit, "out"; this word, "lost", used this starting bit. This word is food for animals, not for people, so he isn\'t talking to the staff.']), (3, None, [('vika', 'происходит'), ' \'Viktor Petrovich, what is happening in the kitchen?\' Vika uses his first name and father\'s name, which is formal and polite: here she is the manager. This word is "is happening".']), (4, None, [('vika', 'готовятся'), ' \'Why are the dishes being prepared so slowly?\' This word has this ending bit, so the dishes "prepare themselves": that\'s how Russian says "are being prepared". This phrase is "so slowly".']), (5, None, [('barinov', 'рабочем'), ('barinov', 'темпе'), ' \'The cooks are working at the normal working pace.\' This word matches "tempo". This phrase is "work in working tempo", the same root twice, which sounds official. Barinov is stalling.']), (6, None, [('vika', 'Недовольные'), ' \'Unhappy guests are already leaving the restaurant!\' This word is this starting bit plus this word, the opposite of the pleased guests at the banquet. This word is "are leaving", on foot, and this phrase is "out of the restaurant".']), (7, None, [('barinov', 'подождут'), ' \'Let them wait a bit for their hot steaks.\' This phrase is "let them wait". This word is borrowed, and this word is "hot". Barinov doesn\'t care.']), (8, None, [('vika', 'не'), ('vika', 'справляются'), ' \'The waiters cannot cope with such a flow.\' This phrase is the "cope" word, in the multiple. This word is a stream, as of a river; this phrase is "with such a flow".']), (9, None, [('barinov', 'ускорю'), ' \'I will personally go and speed up the team.\' This word is the "personally" he used about the stove. This word, "I will speed up", grows from this word, "soon, quickly". This word is "I will go".']), (10, None, [('vika', 'Сделайте'), ' \'Do it right now, chef.\' This word is "do", the polite command. Vika uses the polite "you" even while giving orders. It sounds firm, and a little weary.']), (11, None, [('barinov', 'через'), ('barinov', 'пять'), ('barinov', 'минут'), ' \'Everything will be ready in five minutes.\' This phrase, "through five minutes", is "in five minutes". After this word, this word becomes this word. Barinov promises, and he has no plan.']), (12, None, [('vika', 'чистую'), ('vika', 'правду'), ' \'I hope you are telling the honest truth.\' This phrase, "clean truth", is back: Vika asked Kostya for it earlier. This word is "I hope". It\'s a polite sentence with an edge to it.'])]